{
  "description": "Shared CRDT merge test vectors. Each vector merges records `a` and `b` (each a `{fields, fieldClocks}` pair) and asserts the field-wise LWW result. Consumed by the TypeScript `merge` (task 0002) and the server-side merge RPC (task 0003) so both implementations stay in lockstep. MERGE RULES the SQL twin must reproduce EXACTLY: (1) For each field the winner is the side whose clock string is lexicographically greater. Clock strings follow the HLC format `<ISO-8601 UTC ms>-<4-digit counter>-<deviceId>`, which is fixed-width up through the counter, so plain string comparison equals logical order. (2) A missing fieldClocks entry is the bottom sentinel — it loses to ANY present clock (legacy rows lose to clocked writes), and a field present on only one side is kept as-is. (3) On a byte-identical clock tie (same origin, or two legacy rows with no clock) the winner is the field whose VALUE has the greater canonical serialization. Canonical serialization = JSON with object keys sorted ascending and arrays in element order (numbers unquoted, strings quoted, booleans `true`/`false`, null `null`); values are compared as STRINGS, so e.g. amountMl 100 loses to 90 because `\"100\" < \"90\"`. The winning field's clock (if any) is carried into the merged record; a field won on the empty-clock sentinel carries no clock entry.",
